  "notes": "Scenario 1 — Turn a doomed fight into a coordinated finish\n\nQuick start\nHP means remaining health points. In each tick, everyone chooses a move plus a Basic (ordinary attack/heal), an Ultimate (special ability), or no combat. A cooldown counts the wait before reusing an Ultimate. SharedObs shares your teammates' current sightings, not the opponent's planned actions.\n\nThe challenge\nTeam A has Hunter, Rogue and Priest against Team B's Mage, Rogue and Priest. A tick is one simultaneous team decision. Five ticks remain, with Team A leading 18–17 and 20 points needed to win. Team A needs two kills while allowing no more than two deaths. Use the actual terminal Team A reward: +1 SUCCESS, 0 DRAW, -1 FAIL. A win/loss on the final tick still counts; an ongoing zero reward is not a draw.\n\nThe few rules you need\nHunter attacks at range and can Trap an enemy, preventing several future actions. Rogue is a close-range finisher with a powerful Poison Ultimate. Priest can heal; its much larger Ultimate heal must first finish its cooldown. Mage's active Burst makes its damage especially dangerous. Walls can block sight and attacks. All agents choose before the tick resolves: a new stun or death does not erase attacks already accepted that tick.\n\nWhy this situation is interesting\nPriest-A has only 1 HP and cannot save itself from Mage's committed attack in the reference line. Its last heal can still decide which teammate survives. Mage-B looks like an easy kill at 4 HP, but Priest-B can repair that weakness; its Ultimate becomes ready next decision. Rogue-B starts at 36 HP and Priest-B at 94 HP. Simply attacking the weakest enemy misses the team's real problem: stop the rescue, arrange two finishing attacks, and keep enough allies alive to win.\n\nOne successful team sequence\nTick 1: Priest-A Basic-heals Hunter-A OR Rogue-A and Stays. Rogue-A moves South without attacking, using the wall to break Mage's sight. Hunter-A Traps Priest-B and moves East. Team B's Priest heals Mage, Mage attacks Priest-A, and Rogue attacks/chases Hunter.\nTick 2: Priest-A is dead. Rogue-A Stays without attacking. Hunter-A Basic-attacks Mage-B and moves Northeast. This lets Hunter pursue Mage while the chasing Rogue-B moves into Rogue-A's ambush. Priest-B remains trapped.\nTick 3: Rogue-A Ultimates Rogue-B; Hunter-A Basic-attacks Mage-B. Both Stay in the reference sequence. The two kills secure the win even though the unhealed damage dealer also dies. Its already accepted attack still resolves.\n\nWhat the behaviors look like\n- Teammate Modeling: split the two finishing jobs and anticipate the partner's position.\n- Opponent Modeling: anticipate the healer's return and the Rogue's chase.\n- Crowd Control Prioritization: disable the healer before it can undo the damage.\n- Basic Healing Target Prioritization: spend the last heal on a teammate who can finish.\n- Basic Damage Target Prioritization: Hunter keeps working on Mage.\n- Ultimate Damage Target Prioritization: Rogue saves its powerful hit for the enemy Rogue.\n- Tactical Obstacle Exploitation: the wall denies Mage a target.\n- Tactical Positioning: turn pursuit into an ambush.\n- Strategic Sacrifice: accept a teammate's loss when its final action wins the match.\n\nMulti-Step Team-Level Planning: connect the last heal and opening Trap to the later ambush and two simultaneous finishing attacks. This describes the coordinated sequence, not proof that a policy contains an explicit planner.\n\nVerified result and research use\nBoth healing variants win 20–19 after three ticks. Healing Hunter leaves Hunter at about 2.58 HP; healing Rogue leaves Rogue at 20 HP. Priest-B still has two Trap ticks remaining. These outcomes have been reproduced with Reactive TDM ALPHA v2 on this updated setup; the original r34 witness used Reactive TDM v1. They are not guesses about what a learned policy thinks. The scenario is useful for matched ablations of coordination, information use or target selection; success alone does not prove all listed abilities or make this the only solution. Official evaluation scenarios must not be used for training or tuning.\n\nHow to play and source\nLoad this saved scenario, select SharedObs, keep Team A Manual, and use Reactive TDM ALPHA on Team B. Reset repeats the loaded starting state; load a newly saved revision explicitly to use later edits. Current source: scenario_1 r40, based on r39 with Mage-B cooldown corrected to 30 for its fresh five-tick Burst. Other configured agents start dead; their next revival is at the fifth successor if play continues, after this three-tick win."
